;;; A premise `(not (eq …))` matches stored negations, not `neq` —
;;; regression `not-eq-premise` (`corpus/regressions.toml`)
;;; (Q-M1e2.26).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts `(p a)` and `(p b)`, and a rule concluding `(hit ?x ?y)` from two
;;; `p` facts whose arguments are not equal, written `(not (eq ?x ?y))`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(not (eq ?x ?y))` is `(neq ?x ?y)` (Q-M1f.31): the rule fires on `(a, b)`
;;; and `(b, a)`, so root holds `(hit a b)` and `(hit b a)`. Those are the
;;; only `hit` facts the blind enumerator would propose over `p`'s objects
;;; that root does not already hold — none — so H is empty and R is {∅}.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `dc4c0bdc` (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE`, release and debug
;;; alike), the premise was a pattern over the relation `not`, which no state
;;; held a `(not (eq …))` fact of: the rule never fired, root held no `hit`, H
;;; was `(hit a b)` and `(hit b a)`, and R four states, exit 0. R is {∅} now.
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(relation hit Node Node :why "{?1} and {?2} are two nodes")
(p a)
(p b)
(rule r ()
  :match (and (p ?x) (p ?y) (not (eq ?x ?y)))
  :assert (hit ?x ?y)
  :why "{?x} and {?y} are nodes, and not the same")
